(FUN-43 — (gap) a function body written on indented lines is closed by a
 BLANK LINE, not by end of file and not by the body's last period. This is
 the paragraph-break rule of the Basics chapter reaching functions, and it
 is what justifies the one real sameness this section imposes on
 generated programs: every emitted function definition is followed by a
 blank line. `greet` below is closed properly and runs; `never closed`
 is not, so the top-level `Print` after it is read as part of its body
 and never runs — the last line of this program produces no output at
 all. The compiler does warn, on stderr at compile time:
    warning: Function 'never closed' is still open at end of file: its body
    reached EOF with no closing blank line. A function body is closed by a
    blank line (paragraph break), not by EOF, so without one everything
    after the signature is read as part of the body. If statements after
    the body were meant to run at the top level, add a blank line after
    the function body to close it.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox FUN-43.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   hello
   the closed body did not swallow this
)
To greet.
  Print "hello".

greet.
Print "the closed body did not swallow this".

To 'never closed'.
  Print "this line is inside the body".
Print "this top-level line was swallowed".
